//
// The game's seam — plan 19 phase P1.
//
// Plan 08 V0 gave the EDITOR one door: every action a player can take
// runs through `editor_step`, so a `.keys` script and a keypress are
// the same machine.  This is that rule one level up, for the GAME.
//
// Before this file, the game had no door at all.  `WaveState` rode on
// `ScriptRun` and `wave_tick` was called from two places inside the
// script runner — which was fine only because there was exactly one
// caller in the world.  The moment a GL loop advances the game itself,
// **a script and a player play different games**, and the 520
// measurements in `scripts/validate.sh` stop describing what a person
// experiences.  Silently: every one of them would still be green.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ TWO ways to ask, ONE tick ────────────────────────────────
//
// A script says `tick 30`.  A frame says *16 ms passed*.  Those are
// different QUESTIONS — a count and a duration — and P1 measured that
// they cannot be made one:
//
//     play_advance(n * TICK_SECONDS)  is not  n ticks
//
// **It is wrong for 602 of the first 1000 values of `n`** (measured,
// plan 19 P1), losing the last tick at n = 10, 12, 40, 50, 120, 200,
// 500 and 595 others.  The subtraction chain is what does it, not the
// product: n = 12 gives a product of exactly 8.0 and still comes back
// 11, because eleven subtractions of a repeating fraction leave a
// remainder a hair under the twelfth.
//
// ⚠⚠ **P0 said the opposite, and P0 was measuring n = 30.**  Thirty is
// in the lucky 40%.  Its real finding survives and is narrower: the
// frame SIZE does not reach the simulation, because the remainder
// CARRIES — 1200 frames of 1/60 s and one frame of 20 s reach the same
// state.  What does not survive is the generalisation from one `n`.
//
// So the game has one TICK and two ways to ask for it:
//
//   * `play_ticks(n)`   — a COUNT.  What `tick` and `fall` ask, and
//                         what every one of the 520 gate measurements
//                         is pinned to.  Exact by construction.
//   * `play_advance(s)` — a DURATION.  What a GL frame asks, and the
//                         only one that banks a remainder.
//
// Both spend `play_one_tick`, which is the one site in the repo that
// calls `wave_tick`.  That is the invariant; a single entry point was
// never it, and reaching for one would have made the corpus lie.
//
// ── ⚠⚠ And there is no epsilon, which is a DECISION ──────────────
//
// `helper.loft`, `tower.loft` and `vehicle.loft` all carry one, and a
// reader arriving from any of them will want to add a fourth.  Do not.
// Those are ONE-SHOT timers: their boundary is decided once and for
// ever, so a float landing an ulp short costs a whole tick permanently.
// This one REPEATS, and its remainder CARRIES — an ulp short defers a
// tick to the next frame and nothing is lost.  1200 frames of 1/60 s
// bank 20.000000000000146 s rather than 20.0, and it makes no
// difference at all: the excess is 1.5e-13 against a tick of 0.667.
// Measured with an epsilon of 1e-9 as well, and the state is identical.
//
// ⚠ An epsilon would NOT have rescued the count, either — it moves
// which 602 values are wrong, not how many.  A count is exact because
// it is counted, not because the arithmetic was nudged.
//
// ── ⚠ Why `PlayState` does not own the `EditorState` ─────────────
//
// The plan's own recommendation was a `PlayState` composing an
// `EditorState` and a `WaveState`.  Measured, the first half cannot be
// written: **a struct stored in a field of another struct is a COPY**,
// not an alias (loft says so itself — `advice[avoidable-copy]`).  So a
// `PlayState` built around the session the caller already holds would
// fork the world in two: the script runner mutates `s`, the game reads
// `ps.ed`, and the two drift with nothing to see it.
//
// A field read as a PARAMETER does alias, so owning it outright would
// work — but only by inverting `script_run(s, …)`, whose whole contract
// is that the caller keeps the session and asserts on it afterwards.
//
// So the world is PASSED and the game is OWNED.  `PlayState` is what a
// live session has that an edited one does not: a roster, a clock, and
// the seconds banked toward the next tick.
//
// ── ⚠ And a `tick` is not a FRAME either ────────────────────────
//
// `play_step` is a FRAME — input, then time — and it is what the GL
// loop and every scripted frame call.  `play_ticks` is the simulation
// alone, and it is what `tick` and `fall` call.
//
// That split is not tidiness either.  A script's `tick 30` has no
// keypress in it; routed through `play_step` it would have to fabricate
// a neutral input frame, and `editor_step` reads a neutral frame as
// *the player let go* — it commits the in-flight paint stroke and
// resets the edge history.  `tick` would silently release the player's
// mouse.
//
// What the one-seam rule demands is that ONE function calls
// `wave_tick`.  One does.

use lattice;
use markers;
use editor_step;
use occupancy;
use carry;
use vehicle;
use spawn;
use render_camera;

// ─── State ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A game in progress.
//
// Deliberately NOT on `EditorState`: a session that is being edited has
// no enemies in it, and folding a roster into the seam would make
// `editor_step` answer for something no editor action touches.  That
// argument is `ScriptRun.wave`'s, and it survives the move — this is
// the same record, given a name and a clock so that something other
// than a script can hold one.
pub struct PlayState {
    // The roster, the layers, the towers, the wallet, the crew, the
    // cargo — everything the simulation owns.
    wave: WaveState,

    // Elapsed seconds not yet spent as whole ticks.
    //
    // ⚠ **The remainder is kept, and that is the whole design.**  A
    // frame that delivers 0.4 s takes no tick and banks 0.4; the next
    // one takes a tick and banks the rest.  Zeroing it here — or
    // rounding to the nearest tick — makes the simulation frame-rate
    // dependent, which is the property P0 measured and the one no
    // measurement in this repo would survive losing.
    banked: float,

    // Whole ticks played, ever.  The run's CLOCK — plan 12 B7 compares
    // it between a base with defences and the same base without, and
    // plans 13-17 are built on those comparisons.
    //
    // ⚠ **A count of TICKS, not of seconds** (plan 19 § Open questions
    // 3).  The accumulator changes who decides WHEN a tick happens; it
    // does not change what one IS.
    ticks: integer,

    // Last frame's input, for the play actions' rising edges (plan 19
    // P2).  ⚠ **Its own record, never `EditorState.prev`**: that one is
    // written at the END of `editor_step`, so a play action reading it
    // would compare this frame against itself.  Same shape, same
    // `editor_input_copy`, one frame apart.
    prev: EditorInput,

    // Is this session LIVE — the game running, or the map being edited?
    // Plan 19 P3.  ⚠ See § The mode is remembered by the SESSION for why
    // it is here and not a local in `main()`.
    playing: boolean,

    // The camera the session is looking through, and the boom the
    // player has asked for (plan 21 R2, `@X014`).
    //
    // ⚠ **Here rather than on `EditorState.cam`**, which is
    // `EditorCamera` — a hex position and an unwired zoom (`@D002`) —
    // and belongs to the editor.  This one is a property of a LIVE
    // session, the same argument P3 made for `playing`, and it is the
    // reason `docs/RENDERER.md` § Open 4 was decided rather than asked.
    //
    // ⚠ It arrived one phase after the camera itself, deliberately:
    // until the ease existed there was nothing for a session to
    // REMEMBER between frames, and a field nobody reads is a decision
    // validated by nothing.
    cam: CameraRig,
}

pub fn play_state_new() -> PlayState {
    PlayState {
        wave:    wave_state_empty(),
        banked:  0.0,
        ticks:   0,
        prev:    editor_input_empty(),
        // ⚠ A session opens in the EDITOR, which is what keeps every
        // caller written before P3 — the script runner, and all 28 gate
        // scenarios — meaning exactly what it meant.
        playing: false,
        cam:     camera_rig_new(),
    }
}

// ─── Where the wave is walking to ───────────────────────────────

// How many target markers the world has, and where the last one is.
//
// The core is a MARKER, so the answer lives with the markers rather
// than with whoever is asking — the script runner asked this question
// privately for six plans, and a GL loop needs the same answer.
//
// A count rather than a nullable hex, because "no core" and "three
// cores" are different mistakes and a caller that can only see
// `null` reports the wrong one.
pub fn play_core(mw: const MarkerWorld) -> (integer, integer, integer) {
    pc_n = 0;
    pc_q = 0;
    pc_r = 0;
    for pc_m in mw.markers {
        if pc_m.kind == MARKER_KIND_TARGET as u8 {
            pc_n += 1;
            pc_q = pc_m.q;
            pc_r = pc_m.r;
        }
    }
    (pc_n, pc_q, pc_r)
}

// ─── The mode: editing, or playing (plan 19 P3) ─────────────────
//
// ⚠⚠ **The mode is remembered by the SESSION, not by `main()`.**
//
// `plans/19` § Open questions 2 recommended a flag in the SHELL, and
// P2 built the seam's half exactly that way: `editor_input_from`'s
// `playing` is a per-frame parameter and nothing in the seam remembers
// it.  That half stands.  What moved is where the flag LIVES between
// frames — a local in `main()` is a decision no test can reach, and
// `CLAUDE.md` § Relative paths is blunt about why that matters here:
// an entry point carrying `#cwd` cannot be `use`d, so `main.loft` is
// compiled by nothing and must stay a shell with no decisions in it.
//
// So the seam is still parameterised — nothing is baked in, and the
// landing flow plan 05 will bring can set this flag instead of a key —
// but the flag itself is game state, where CI can see it.
//
// ⚠ It gates the CLOCK and NEVER the seam.  `in_playing` says what the
// KEYS mean this frame; `playing` says whether wall time reaches the
// simulation at all.  Confusing the two would break the corpus: every
// P1 and P2 test hands `play_step` real seconds on a session that has
// never been in play mode, because a scripted frame's time is the
// SCRIPT's business and always 0.0 anyway.

pub fn play_mode(ps: const PlayState) -> boolean {
    ps.playing
}

// How much of a frame's elapsed time the GAME gets.
//
// ⚠ **This is the whole of what the mode does to the clock**, and it is
// a function rather than an `if` in the GL loop so that a gate can see
// it: `tests/19_p3_the_clock.loft` § An edit session has no clock is the
// assertion, and without it the rule would live only in `main.loft`,
// where nothing compiles it.
//
// ⚠ Time spent editing is DROPPED rather than banked — the same
// decision `play_advance` makes about a coreless world, and for the
// same reason: banking it would make pressing P replay the whole
// editing session as one burst of ticks.
pub fn play_frame_seconds(ps: const PlayState, elapsed: float) -> float {
    if ps.playing { elapsed } else { 0.0 }
}

// The run starts: put the crew on the ground at the core.
//
// ⚠ **This is a STAND-IN for plan 05's landing flow, and deliberately
// the smallest one.**  The design lands the player on a spot they pick
// with the mouse; picking one is UI work `plans/19` § What this plan
// does NOT build excludes.  The core is the one hex a run is guaranteed
// to have, so it is where the crew appears until that flow exists.
//
// ⚠ **Only when there is nobody there.**  Leaving play mode and coming
// back must not teleport the player home — the state is kept across the
// toggle, so the toggle is a pause rather than a restart.
//
// Answers whether it placed anybody.
pub fn play_begin(ps: PlayState, s: EditorState) -> boolean {
    if vehicle_present(ps.wave.player) { return false; }
    // ⚠ No core, no run — the same `play_core` both doors ask, so a
    // half-built map cannot start a game any more than it can tick one.
    pb_core = play_core(s.mw);
    if pb_core.0 != 1 { return false; }
    vehicle_place(ps.wave.player, pb_core.1, pb_core.2);
    true
}

// Enter or leave play mode.
//
// ⚠ Idempotent, because the entry does WORK: a caller that set the flag
// on every frame it was already set would re-run `play_begin` — which is
// harmless today only because `play_begin` refuses an occupied chassis.
// Two guards is one too few to rely on.
pub fn play_set_mode(ps: PlayState, s: EditorState, on: boolean) {
    if ps.playing == on { return; }
    ps.playing = on;
    if on { play_begin(ps, s); }
}

// ─── The play actions (plan 19 P2) ──────────────────────────────

// ⚠⚠ **WASD is a COMPASS heading, resolved in METRES — there is no
// direction table here and there must not be one.**
//
// `DESIGN.md` § The handful of keys says WASD moves.  dryopea is
// pointy-top odd-r, whose six neighbours are E, SE, SW, W, NW and NE —
// **there is no due-north neighbour at all**, so the four keys cannot
// be four `lat_neighbour` directions.  Three ways out were open and
// two of them invent something:
//
//   * a (key -> lattice direction) table: W would have to mean NE, so
//     holding "north" would drift steadily east and the player would
//     feel it.  It also resurrects the constant direction table
//     `lattice.loft` deliberately does not have.
//   * drive to the hovered hex: reproduces the existing `drive <q> <r>`
//     verb exactly and costs nothing — but `DESIGN.md` § 11 reserves
//     the mouse for UI clicks and gives movement to WASD.
//   * **this**: the keys name a heading in METRES, the heading is added
//     to the vehicle's metre position, and `lat_from_metres` names the
//     hex.  W really is north, the four keys reach all eight compass
//     headings through their combinations, and the arithmetic is
//     `lattice.loft`'s own — including the y-negation that makes the
//     compass true on dryopea's screen.
//
// ⚠ So a heading is (x, y) in metres with **+y SOUTH**, matching
// `lat_to_metres`' frame and the canvas'.  Not normalised: a diagonal
// is longer than an axis by √2, which only makes the target further
// away, and the target is re-pointed every frame anyway.
fn play_heading(input: const EditorInput) -> (float, float) {
    ph_x = 0.0;
    ph_y = 0.0;
    if input.in_drive_east  { ph_x += 1.0; }
    if input.in_drive_west  { ph_x -= 1.0; }
    if input.in_drive_south { ph_y += 1.0; }
    if input.in_drive_north { ph_y -= 1.0; }
    (ph_x, ph_y)
}

// How far ahead a held direction points, in hexes.
//
// ⚠ **Derived from the vehicle, not chosen.**  It only has to exceed
// what one tick can cover, because the target is re-pointed every
// frame: shorter and a hold would stall on arrival, longer and a
// RELEASE would coast further before § the stop takes effect.  A
// boosting vehicle covers `vehicle_hexes_this_tick`, so one more than
// that is the smallest honest answer — and it tracks a boost, which a
// constant would not.
fn play_steer_reach(v: const Vehicle) -> integer {
    vehicle_hexes_this_tick(v, TICK_SECONDS) + 1
}

// Where a held heading points, as a hex.
fn play_steer_target(v: const Vehicle, hx: float, hy: float) -> Hex {
    pst_here  = lat_to_metres(Hex { q: v.q, r: v.r });
    pst_reach = (play_steer_reach(v) as float) * HEX_FLAT_TO_FLAT;
    lat_from_metres(pst_here.0 + hx * pst_reach, pst_here.1 + hy * pst_reach)
}

// Apply one frame of PLAY intent — what `editor_step` cannot, because
// the editor's seam has no roster, no vehicle and no cargo.
//
// ⚠ **Nothing happens on an EDIT frame.**  `in_playing` is what says
// which, and without that guard the release branch below would stop a
// vehicle that a script had pointed somewhere with `drive <q> <r>` —
// silently, on the next camera pan, in every scenario in the gate.
fn play_actions(ps: PlayState, s: EditorState, input: const EditorInput) {
    if !input.in_playing { return; }
    if !vehicle_present(ps.wave.player) { return; }

    // ── Steering ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // ⚠ **Held, not edge-triggered, and re-pointed EVERY frame.**  The
    // vehicle carries a destination rather than a velocity, so a held
    // key is a destination kept just ahead of it.  Re-pointing from the
    // vehicle's CURRENT hex is what makes the result frame-rate
    // independent: the frame decides where to aim, `vehicle_tick`
    // decides how fast.
    pa_head = play_heading(input);
    if pa_head.0 != 0.0 || pa_head.1 != 0.0 {
        pa_to = play_steer_target(ps.wave.player, pa_head.0, pa_head.1);
        vehicle_drive(ps.wave.player, pa_to.q, pa_to.r);
    } else {
        // ⚠ **Releasing STOPS it**, rather than letting it coast to a
        // destination the player has stopped asking for.  Stopping is
        // spelled *drive to where you already are*, so there is still
        // exactly one way to set a destination.
        vehicle_drive(ps.wave.player, ps.wave.player.q, ps.wave.player.r);
    }

    // ── Boost ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    // ⚠ **Edge-triggered, though `DESIGN.md` calls it a held key.**
    // Held is how it FEELS — the lift lasts 2 s whatever the finger
    // does — but `vehicle_boost` is a one-shot that refuses while
    // boosting or cooling, so a held Shift would silently re-arm the
    // instant the 5 s cooldown expired.  That is a second boost the
    // player did not decide to spend, and `DESIGN.md` § The
    // opportunity-cost layer is the whole reason spending one is a
    // decision.
    if input.in_boost && !ps.prev.in_boost {
        vehicle_boost(ps.wave.player);
    }

    // ── Carry: ONE key, resolved on what the hands hold ─────────
    //
    // ⚠ Edge-triggered for the obvious reason — a held E would pick a
    // thing up and put it down again every frame.
    //
    // ⚠ **The order is the design's, not a coin toss**: `wave_take`
    // first, and only a carrier with full hands deposits.  That is what
    // makes the key *context-resolved on the carrier's state* rather
    // than on what happens to be underfoot.
    if input.in_carry && !ps.prev.in_carry {
        if cargo_carrying(ps.wave.cargo, BLOCKER_PLAYER) {
            pa_core = play_core(s.mw);
            if pa_core.0 == 1 {
                wave_drop(ps.wave, BLOCKER_PLAYER,
                          Hex { q: pa_core.1, r: pa_core.2 }, s.mw);
            }
        } else {
            wave_take(ps.wave, BLOCKER_PLAYER, s.mw);
        }
    }
}

// ─── The tick ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

// One tick of the game.
//
// ⚠ **The ONE caller of `wave_tick` in the whole repo.**  That is the
// invariant this file exists to hold: a GL loop that ticked the game
// itself, or a script verb that kept its own loop, would be a second
// game wearing the same numbers.  Both doors below reach the
// simulation through here and nowhere else.
fn play_one_tick(ps: PlayState, s: EditorState, at: Hex) {
    wave_tick(ps.wave, s.picker.palette, s.pw, s.mw, at);
    ps.ticks += 1;
}

// ─── The two doors ──────────────────────────────────────────────

// Play exactly `n` ticks, and answer how many that was.
//
// ⚠ **A COUNT, and it stays a count all the way down** — never `n`
// converted to seconds and handed to `play_advance`, which is wrong for
// 602 of the first 1000 values of `n` (§ TWO ways to ask).  The 520
// measurements in `scripts/validate.sh` are pinned to exact tick
// counts, and `a-base-that-plays-its-list` is the scenario that says
// so out loud: through the seconds door its first wave never arrives.
//
// ⚠ **It does not touch `banked`.**  A count is not elapsed time, so
// it neither spends a live session's remainder nor leaves one behind.
pub fn play_ticks(ps: PlayState, s: EditorState, n: integer) -> integer {
    // ⚠ **No core, no game.**  A half-built map has no target marker
    // and the wave has nothing to walk towards.
    pt_core = play_core(s.mw);
    if pt_core.0 != 1 { return 0; }
    pt_at = Hex { q: pt_core.1, r: pt_core.2 };

    for _ in 0..n { play_one_tick(ps, s, pt_at); }
    if n > 0 { n } else { 0 }
}

// Spend elapsed time on the game, and answer how many whole ticks that
// bought.
//
// ⚠ **No clamp on the tick count, deliberately.**  A frame that
// delivers 20 s really does play 30 ticks.  A clamp would make the
// simulation frame-rate dependent at whatever length it chose — the one
// property P0 proved this shape has and the one no measurement in the
// repo would survive losing.
pub fn play_advance(ps: PlayState, s: EditorState, seconds: float) -> integer {
    // ⚠ **No core, no game — and the seconds are DROPPED, not banked.**
    // A half-built map has no target marker, and banking through it
    // would make placing the core fire every second that had passed
    // since the session opened as one burst of ticks.
    pa_core = play_core(s.mw);
    if pa_core.0 != 1 { return 0; }
    pa_at = Hex { q: pa_core.1, r: pa_core.2 };

    ps.banked += seconds;
    pa_taken = 0;
    // ⚠ No epsilon on this comparison — see § And there is no epsilon.
    while ps.banked >= TICK_SECONDS {
        ps.banked -= TICK_SECONDS;
        play_one_tick(ps, s, pa_at);
        pa_taken += 1;
    }
    pa_taken
}

// One frame: what the player did, then how long it took.
//
// The GL loop calls this with what is held and the seconds since the
// last frame; a scripted frame calls it with the keys the script
// pressed and 0.0.  Both get the same machine, which is the point.
//
// ⚠ **Input first, then time.**  A key pressed this frame acts on the
// world the ticks then play out over — the same ordering `wave_tick`
// keeps internally, where everything the tick reads is fixed before
// anybody moves.  Ticking first would make a keypress arrive one frame
// late at 60 fps and thirty ticks late at 1.
//
// ⚠ And the EDITOR's half goes before the GAME's, so a play action
// reads the world an editor action has already changed — the same
// reason, one level down.
//
// ⚠ **`seconds` is what the CALLER decided the game gets**, not the
// wall clock's raw answer.  A GL loop passes `play_frame_seconds(ps,
// elapsed)` so that an edited session is frozen; a script passes 0.0
// because its clock is `tick`.  This function does not consult the mode
// for the time — see § The mode is remembered by the SESSION.
pub fn play_step(ps: PlayState, s: EditorState, input: EditorInput,
                 seconds: float) -> integer {
    editor_step(s, input);
    play_actions(ps, s, input);
    // ⚠ **The mode flips at the END of the frame, never at its start.**
    // The input was resolved under the OLD mode — WASD already meant one
    // of its two things before this line could run — so flipping first
    // would make one frame's keys and that frame's mode disagree.  The
    // press lands; the next frame is the new mode.
    //
    // ⚠ And it is here rather than in the shell because `ps.prev` is
    // overwritten on the next line: a caller doing this outside would
    // have to save the previous frame first, and a caller that forgot
    // would toggle on every frame the key was held.
    if input.in_toggle_play && !ps.prev.in_toggle_play {
        play_set_mode(ps, s, !ps.playing);
    }
    // ⚠ **Recorded on EVERY frame, including edit frames and frames
    // with no vehicle** — `play_actions` returns early from both, and
    // a `prev` that skipped them would leave a key looking held across
    // the gap, firing an edge the player never made.  The same rule
    // `editor_step`'s own `s.prev` keeps, and for the same reason.
    ps.prev = editor_input_copy(input);
    ps_ticks = play_advance(ps, s, seconds);

    // ⚠⚠ **The camera is stepped on EVERY frame, and it is stepped
    // LAST.**
    //
    // Last, because a camera reads the world the ticks just produced —
    // the same *input, then time* ordering one layer down, with the eye
    // arriving after both.
    //
    // Every frame, because the ease is a function of ELAPSED TIME and
    // nothing else.  ⚠ Putting this inside `play_advance`'s tick loop is
    // the mistake to avoid: at 60 fps it would run on one frame in
    // forty, and the result would be a camera that stutters with the
    // right average — which reads as a frame-rate problem rather than
    // as a camera one.  It is also moros's shipped fault in dryopea's
    // shape (`render_camera.loft` § camera_rig_step).
    //
    // ⚠ `seconds` is the SAME seconds the simulation got, so an edit
    // session's camera is frozen exactly as its clock is — the mode
    // gates one number and everything downstream of it.
    camera_rig_step(ps.cam, ps.wave.player, s.picker.palette, s.pw,
                    ps.wave.heights, seconds);
    ps_ticks
}
